WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.428
Schema migrations are versioned changes applied in a known order.

00:00:03.578 --> 00:00:07.457
Once a migration has been applied in a shared environment, add a

00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:09.397
new migration rather than editing history.

00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:15.125
Hibernate schema validation checks mapping compatibility; automatic schema update is not a

00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:17.450
replacement for reviewed production migrations.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.924
Use expand-and-contract changes for rolling deployments.

00:00:21.074 --> 00:00:25.601
First add a compatible field or table, then deploy code that can

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:30.127
handle both states, backfill safely, switch reads, and finally remove obsolete structures

00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:32.013
after old code is gone.

00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:37.369
Large backfills need progress tracking, bounded batches, and operational monitoring.

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:41.479
Flyway creates each service’s schema from its own migration directory.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:46.616
The default H2 profile makes the course easy to run, while the

00:00:46.616 --> 00:00:49.525
PostgreSQL profile accepts an external database connection.

00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:54.463
The two services must use separate databases or isolated ownership boundaries; sharing

00:00:54.463 --> 00:00:57.257
table access would undermine the service split.

00:00:57.407 --> 00:01:01.770
Add a nullable customer reference to orders, backfill synthetic rows, then make

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.952
it required in a later migration.

00:01:04.102 --> 00:01:07.562
Test both a fresh database and an upgrade from the previous schema.

00:01:07.712 --> 00:01:10.693
A fresh-install test alone misses upgrade failures.

00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:16.368
Preserve old migration checksums, test against production-like data volume, and plan rollback

00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:17.749
or forward repair.

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:22.354
A destructive migration may not be reversible by simply redeploying old Java

00:01:22.354 --> 00:01:22.726
code.

00:01:22.876 --> 00:01:23.547
Question.

00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:27.542
Why test migrations on PostgreSQL if H2 passes?

00:01:27.692 --> 00:01:28.269
Answer.

00:01:28.419 --> 00:01:33.995
SQL dialect, locking, types, indexes, and execution plans can differ.

00:01:34.145 --> 00:01:34.816
Question.

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:37.244
What makes a rolling schema change safe?

00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:37.971
Answer.

00:01:38.121 --> 00:01:42.608
Old and new application versions can both operate correctly during the transition.
